  "recovery_latency_s": 124.59549808502197,
  "client_view_text": " When a database system crashes, its primary goal is to recover the database to a consistent state, which means recovering the data in a state where it is recoverable and can be used by the database system. The primary component of this recovery process involves the database system reading its log files to rebuild its database to a recoverable state. A write-ahead log is a key component that serves this purpose. When a transaction is committed or rolled back in a database system, the database system records the transaction's outcome in a write-ahead log. This log is a sequence of operations that represent the changes to the database as a sequence of write operations, operations to delete data, and operations to read data. The write-ahead log provides a record of all changes that were made to the database before the crash, including all transactions that were committed or rolled back. When the database system recovers after a crash, the database system reads its write-ahead log and re-applies the operations recorded in the log to the current database state. The result is a consistent database state that can be used by the database system. The write-ahead log ensures that the database system can recover even if it crashes after a transaction has been partially executed. If the database system crashed, the undo entries for those partially executed transactions can"
}